#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161776 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/id #uuid "122fd33e-5ef4-5200-9c72-67f40f3b8ca0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8168" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2?$\n\n![](images/52/528416f6f9bd58e04afc6df73ddffbe1e8645f1e94fc7113d1458fb806f08713.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ has an associated $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $L$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since it's a $3 \\times 3$ matrix (not $2 \\times 2$).\n\n- $M$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2.$  Although it's a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, $M$ is not symmetric since $m_{12} \\neq m_{21}.$\n\n- $N$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since it is a $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$N$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161777 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161985 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f62dc8-396d-5f7f-8295-2e7b5d62bd78" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Quadratic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "d782c826-1111-5c83-98b6-220856d74b41" :question/math-academy-id "e-8169" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f85f2ebf91733fa3b5f0b6080da27be0ee4333ee2b7b27ab3fac2dad1acb2d41.svg)\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ when ![](images/81/815a776cae60244113513dc20183d05caeece6aaef807fd65e5c4ac819f2d4d3.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Substituting ![](images/e8/e8a68c7428583d7df9f7fb18faf50f315192475e2f9bb12d051ccc1fda5638a3.svg) into the expression for $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 23\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =86.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161778 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161986 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "328b0b77-f789-581e-a119-9823297666ae" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Down a Quadratic Form Given Its Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "f3be44df-f059-5e92-b279-644fe1eb0985" :question/math-academy-id "e-8170" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0aa2d0bbe2e39ba23901b5157466b4099c1f2552d9a34de9af6387975c59e1cb.svg)\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which expression represents the corresponding quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the components of ![](images/37/37cee7d28f0085ee70cefd5cb9ded630e38ffb3ca26523c320df13d09cde09e8.svg)" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry $a_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term $a_{ij}x_i x_j$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =-1{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+(1){x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(1){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+-1{x}_{2}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}(-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+{x}_{2}({x}_{1}-{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161779 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161987 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb93c0ea-26d9-5190-9677-b8c7eb6f7ef1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "9ed75397-5920-5326-a5b9-7537e30f0a85" :question/math-academy-id "e-8171" :question/problem "![](images/36/36f35d430db979991c566198b6126c940d2b5e35682edbabffb458a77b991488.svg)\nThe matrix of the quadratic form ![](images/16/16124e0080293b4221a1fbe668c41d2c293ebe6780025a433530a48ef7047063.svg) is given above. What is the value of ![](images/43/43b267893b1bca2af9d56014fddd4b93a381f8e2c827cb88e6ba2269f1425432.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n![](images/38/38d71b4d0e2e6e4e271e04889292fc77209655fd96accd7ab94358839527c381.svg)\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must do the following splits:\n\n- we split the coefficient of ![](images/3c/3cd51b9ca52bf7654f66792b03bd448a08b1122b07fb9028abf3cf9eb317691e.svg) between the entries ![](images/04/041786e547d7190e7dc272dcce677ae110c98a1c27601d701527c08a27ce069f.svg) and\n\n- we split the coefficient of ![](images/82/824c1904e6a2dbe749c4a8c6dba76e5f1f3470e44c549f9fe472f11b871b1fe5.svg) between the entries ![](images/e2/e2035cd0dc43c754085c2be1daf4394ebe91d59706bc609c2398323dc160edee.svg)\n\nSo, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n![](images/ff/ffc81faf6d2f06bbe8d74cd6b8fc69e9cd1d38a330f492bf925441633ca283e5.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/5d/5db60b1276b1838de62d9a1fa07bdb79930a293720bc5e88ab3113863282afeb.svg) ![](images/af/af43e412255aa69feb8f127bcba2b8aa43eb5d5efb12d692f4f4e2d49c035a98.svg) and ![](images/18/187bf10c65391a58d6b69f746f7f2c4cf2939b3beacfc02c15cef309875c3b57.svg) Finally, \n![](images/af/af56151e2bfb52d48f7b45d954f8c91cbaa1831fe024245667f4f4d721c87e6f.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355ee90c-29a8-5f7d-9858-d5d774afc427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464144} {:answer/id #uuid "52c13329-ed29-5a6a-9c3a-980f9b642801" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "355ee90c-29a8-5f7d-9858-d5d774afc427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464144} :answer-field/id #uuid "57268d9e-f878-521e-88c1-e38ad05c4b25" :answer-field/key "R" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465006} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fca2a6a-5f0e-57c2-b88a-83793aa0ea03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464146} {:answer/id #uuid "d9140ca1-8724-5815-b59d-81a85f001cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fca2a6a-5f0e-57c2-b88a-83793aa0ea03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464146} :answer-field/id #uuid "d672360c-79a2-53e5-89fd-18eb4ebb73b0" :answer-field/key "S" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465007} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "729a3d56-7c5c-50c8-b093-a5ba7c6c0128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464148} {:answer/id #uuid "a192252a-3f86-5dc5-852c-3bafb620daf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a192252a-3f86-5dc5-852c-3bafb620daf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464149} :answer-field/id #uuid "045f8e77-8eca-5ea2-86b3-e877ff4aa8cc" :answer-field/key "T" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d68a509c-d790-5483-ab3b-63aef29c3b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-140870" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,S=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,T=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $R$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $3\\times 3$).\n\n- $S$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}.$ Although it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $S$ is not symmetric since ${s}_{23}\\ne {s}_{32}.$\n\n- $T$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161986 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "328b0b77-f789-581e-a119-9823297666ae" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Down a Quadratic Form Given Its Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a0f2148-7ade-5ec8-ab86-c7e431d919fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269940} {:answer/id #uuid "5ffb9214-474e-5372-a1eb-174b26aa7439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270117} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4c2346-7df9-5410-9d87-366e1e19b329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270681} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9b028c-108b-526f-a4e8-1e7cb18ddf10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271214} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ae9565-9694-58a6-aab9-151038d2b131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f4c2346-7df9-5410-9d87-366e1e19b329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270681} :answer-field/id #uuid "065e2a25-744f-55d4-9b51-be1e062d5b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5a533cc-6fd5-51dd-a1d8-7511844d6151" :question/math-academy-id "q-140928" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =1{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(-1){x}_{1}{x}_{3}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+0{x}_{2}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+(-1){x}_{3}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2}+0{x}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({x}_{1}-{x}_{3})+{x}_{2}(3{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}(-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}{x}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+6{x}_{2}{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161985 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f62dc8-396d-5f7f-8295-2e7b5d62bd78" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03315f4c-29f9-5335-bf32-1a4c56087564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269297} {:answer/id #uuid "7c208796-7c48-534f-b951-cdeb5025345e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270358} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6dfe0c-feda-5664-9df0-54ac9c230c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270488} {:answer/id #uuid "92037151-e69a-5500-b6e8-690e4fe8c09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270544} {:answer/id #uuid "9c96fb7b-b378-527b-a9cb-ae21c483e8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03315f4c-29f9-5335-bf32-1a4c56087564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269297} :answer-field/id #uuid "685add4a-af6c-5961-9669-2663c3076fb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95eb1ba0-88cc-5104-bc0d-3a93ab6de4cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140929" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-3{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-3{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-4\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 3\\cdot 0-3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =9+12+0+0 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161985 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f62dc8-396d-5f7f-8295-2e7b5d62bd78" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61668a4d-a9aa-528d-b42b-fd1b72e2077b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490691} {:answer/id #uuid "7d18a3f9-2fca-582b-aa0a-8054fbaae593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490759} {:answer/id #uuid "93089472-c827-5a81-9873-0f6756288ef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490810} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2388f2-6372-5def-9569-eccffa2b439d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490901} {:answer/id #uuid "f20ac334-93ac-5271-98aa-6f85b9667d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f20ac334-93ac-5271-98aa-6f85b9667d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490972} :answer-field/id #uuid "f799ac64-6ac4-5678-be0b-90e21732933a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80dc5c66-c7d3-5e30-acc4-5e4e63237441" :question/math-academy-id "q-140930" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ into the expression for $Q(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161987 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb93c0ea-26d9-5190-9677-b8c7eb6f7ef1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09186c66-3df7-5958-bab9-d6eceedcf24b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269352} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc9401d-5750-5d84-973f-2999e18b0e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269498} {:answer/id #uuid "43ef2164-f9ee-520c-ad40-88d74fa2b378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269883} {:answer/id #uuid "4d695520-7dd1-5bf7-a0f0-916f7513d13d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269969} {:answer/id #uuid "5dddb6f7-c5c2-5d95-b52f-aa67277d9998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dddb6f7-c5c2-5d95-b52f-aa67277d9998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270103} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fa92d2a-c490-5653-aff1-56f027732c5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bcdcd1b6-5a07-5e6a-b243-0a76c6534f4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140932" :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ b & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & =2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}_{1}^{2}+(-2){x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,(-2){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must split the coefficient of $-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries \n\n$$\n{a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{(-4)}{2}=-2\n$$\n\nof our matrix. So, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2$ and $b=-2.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=2+(-2)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161986 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "328b0b77-f789-581e-a119-9823297666ae" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Down a Quadratic Form Given Its Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37bed92b-f672-507f-908f-4180d60967a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355166} {:answer/id #uuid "7a4e148e-4cda-58e5-a8f8-33882e3c14d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355735} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd54d13-8fee-5d25-aa21-73e2fe837e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356326} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4a3363-52f8-53e5-b467-965e5d801060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356471} {:answer/id #uuid "de2c0735-3990-56ef-921f-e2e38cf38440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37bed92b-f672-507f-908f-4180d60967a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355166} :answer-field/id #uuid "24c65efe-f0dc-5833-85bd-c0e38f31a3b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef8e1919-faae-510c-bb46-8c3989805365" :question/math-academy-id "q-93705" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following represents the corresponding quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in terms of the components of $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry ${a}_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term ${a}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =1{x}_{1}{x}_{1}+(-2){x}_{1}{x}_{2}+(-2){x}_{2}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{2}{x}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+{x}_{2}(-2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161987 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb93c0ea-26d9-5190-9677-b8c7eb6f7ef1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2d8b0e0-abf7-5804-b4fc-1492f981e973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160875} {:answer/id #uuid "25c5dc3c-74b1-560e-8928-9a38e10da2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160876} {:answer/id #uuid "625c16aa-5f30-5dd2-90ef-fdf2417a41f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160877} {:answer/id #uuid "62f64a99-2dff-56c3-9292-977af8cfde01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160878} {:answer/id #uuid "709af0a8-7e72-5eca-b673-1a1761d01bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62f64a99-2dff-56c3-9292-977af8cfde01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160878} :answer-field/id #uuid "da8601d7-22c5-5de4-8cfd-e028ad48d9e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cedbc16a-539c-525b-a8a8-cb2cc644cfec" :question/math-academy-id "q-93712" :question/problem "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ b & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,4{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must split the coefficient of $8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries \n\n$$\n{a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{8}{2}=4\n$$\n\nof our matrix. So, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1$ and $b=4.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=1+4=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40bedae6-4059-50c6-8adc-c9c715240d0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ and $G$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490635} {:answer/id #uuid "64117217-2030-5e31-84f1-78affc963f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$G$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490699} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf84d03-66d8-568a-9b14-8f2ef4741a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490723} {:answer/id #uuid "77244729-5340-5f5f-9d6e-19fd434da41e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$G$ and $H$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490748} {:answer/id #uuid "b7899cf8-c91b-54f9-9319-7ddeff060c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$F$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64117217-2030-5e31-84f1-78affc963f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$G$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490699} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb8bb339-5bb9-5280-8b25-5509bc02dbb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09cd1679-7967-5d9b-a465-697df00c6bd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-94061" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nF=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,G=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,H=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ has an associated $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $F$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}.$ Although it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix, $F$ is not symmetric since ${f}_{12}\\ne {f}_{21}.$\n\n- $G$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it is a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $H$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{2}$ since it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix (not $2\\times 2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$G$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d31c7c30-6a09-537a-838e-26380db70980" :answer/type {:db/id #e