"F" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61630213-687e-509d-b369-4384ce08371c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94063" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{4}?$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,F=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ has an associated $4\\times 4$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $D$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}.$ Although it's a $4\\times 4$ matrix, $D$ is not symmetric since ${d}_{24}\\ne {d}_{42}.$\n\n- $E$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $4\\times 4$).\n\n- $F$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{4}$ since it is a $4\\times 4$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$F$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161984 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97c683e8-d022-5dee-bad9-35e2cd1ccd89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160989} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a2f5e8-88f2-5ca7-829a-bde5ff808860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160990} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a99bd4-fb67-538c-9ece-a4608cb1871a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$J$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160991} {:answer/id #uuid "fca7d285-ba33-51b7-8ce8-27bc0b3b6983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$J$ and $K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160992} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4a9a25-8245-5a1f-bdb8-7661bfd56e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $J$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f4a9a25-8245-5a1f-bdb8-7661bfd56e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $J$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160993} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd5c6c9-c655-5a1d-9014-f450a69f1b54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6fea8541-6770-5397-b251-59a7fad8f7ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-94065" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $H$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $J$ could also be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $K$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}.$ Although it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $K$ is not symmetric since ${k}_{23}\\ne {k}_{32}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$H$ and $J$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161985 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f62dc8-396d-5f7f-8295-2e7b5d62bd78" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4796e45d-7484-566d-8495-7b810c7b8fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490644} {:answer/id #uuid "69f280b3-a4d1-574d-9810-c3cd4368897e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490711} {:answer/id #uuid "6d75b1d4-e8b4-5c44-b220-f423956a19eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490724} {:answer/id #uuid "a222dab7-5e56-5667-9fba-2c3bf4e00399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490844} {:answer/id #uuid "f712252f-a65c-510b-a4d6-510469142eeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a222dab7-5e56-5667-9fba-2c3bf4e00399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490844} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6c9a87f-4b9f-58f6-8c95-7390a7567e1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64ee7b8f-3e22-5cf5-9caf-3d5b7abcf58b" :question/math-academy-id "q-94071" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic form \n$Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{3}^{2}.$ \nEvaluate $Q(x)$ when $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the expression for $Q(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-3)^{2} \\\\  & =1+2+6+18 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357647 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161985 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f62dc8-396d-5f7f-8295-2e7b5d62bd78" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4453eb-2267-5b35-a0fa-3451bada2711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354758} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec09201-448a-5927-b1d6-d670c2941e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355501} {:answer/id #uuid "b1417d46-6e5f-553f-91ad-535af69d5276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356224} {:answer/id #uuid "da75d9bd-46de-5a72-84a7-b66c5d7f37f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356586} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8c4557-1921-5623-801b-cbf59ef1f2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a4453eb-2267-5b35-a0fa-3451bada2711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354758} :answ